WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.230 --> 00:00:01.850
- [Narrator] Midterm
elections are coming up.

00:00:01.850 --> 00:00:04.300
But does your vote
actually make a difference?

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:06.810
U.S. voter turnout is amongst the lowest

00:00:06.820 --> 00:00:08.440
in the entire developed world.

00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:09.970
And it might be because we feel like

00:00:09.970 --> 00:00:11.840
our individual vote
won't make a difference

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:12.980
in the mass of votes.

00:00:13.090 --> 00:00:14.620
Like one Skittle will make a difference

00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:15.670
in a jar of hundreds.

00:00:15.670 --> 00:00:18.447
But it's time we stop horsing
around and we get to voting.

00:00:18.447 --> 00:00:20.880
'Cause democracy, it's
like pretty important.

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:22.490
You might think one vote
won't make a difference.

00:00:22.490 --> 00:00:23.840
But if you and the person next to you

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:26.030
feel the same way, then
that creates a domino effect

00:00:26.030 --> 00:00:28.430
that could lead to the
loss of a bunch of votes.

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:30.480
Plus who says a few votes
can't make a difference?

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:31.313
Check this out.

00:00:31.390 --> 00:00:36.130
In 2000, George W. Bush won
Florida by only 537 votes.

00:00:36.130 --> 00:00:38.380
About six million votes
were cast in Florida,

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:42.080
which means that he won by only 0.009%

00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:43.610
and this gave him the presidency.

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:47.380
In 1839, Marcus Morton became
the governor of Massachusetts

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:48.320
thanks to one vote.

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:49.750
So like, out of this pool of ducks,

00:00:49.750 --> 00:00:51.100
this one dude made history.

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:53.490
I know it feels like our
votes are microscopic,

00:00:53.490 --> 00:00:55.230
but so is that bacteria
that caused the Black Plague

00:00:55.230 --> 00:00:57.220
and that killed about
25 million people, so

00:00:57.220 --> 00:00:58.970
small things can have a big impact.

00:00:59.330 --> 00:01:02.160
In 1994, two candidates
from Wyoming State House

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:03.980
received the exact same number of votes.

00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:05.400
Randall Luthi won the seat

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:07.360
when his name, written
on a ping pong ball,

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:08.830
was drawn from a cowboy hat.

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:11.990
And again in 2011, Tanya Flanagan

00:01:11.990 --> 00:01:14.470
and Linda Meisenheimer tied
during a primary election

00:01:14.470 --> 00:01:15.620
in North Las Vegas.

00:01:15.649 --> 00:01:17.390
Meisenheimer drew the higher card

00:01:17.390 --> 00:01:19.610
securing her spot in the general election,

00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:21.200
which she went on to lose
but that's not the point.

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:23.090
Another example comes from 2008,

00:01:23.090 --> 00:01:25.980
when Democrat Al Franken
beat Republican Norm Coleman

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:30.300
by 312 votes out of almost
2.9 million votes cast.

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:33.620
312 votes is about 0.011%.

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:34.460
That's like nothing.

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:36.380
And there's so many
other examples for this.

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:37.480
This happens a lot ya'll.

00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:38.730
Especially during midterms.

00:01:38.730 --> 00:01:40.500
Like push your friends
to the voting polls,

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:41.720
bring your aunt and uncle,

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:43.510
your brother, Adam and
his boyfriend, Steve.

00:01:43.510 --> 00:01:45.420
So regardless of what
party you are voting for,

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:46.790
you should get to voting.

00:01:50.413 --> 00:01:52.746
(fun music)

